1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,694
Spin a permanent-magnet motor with nothing
connected,

2
00:00:03,734 --> 00:00:06,686
and a voltage appears across its
terminals.

3
00:00:06,726 --> 00:00:11,133
Spin it twice as fast, and the voltage
doubles, along with its frequency.

4
00:00:11,173 --> 00:00:14,913
That's the back-EMF: the voltage a drive
has to overcome,

5
00:00:14,953 --> 00:00:19,125
which decides how fast the motor can run
on a given supply.

6
00:00:19,165 --> 00:00:22,235
Today we'll compute it from Lesson 5's
field solution,

7
00:00:22,275 --> 00:00:25,137
then measure it with a drill and a scope.

8
00:00:26,087 --> 00:00:28,464
First, the answers from Lesson 6.

9
00:00:28,504 --> 00:00:31,772
One: a five-sixths pitch coil has a

10
00:00:31,812 --> 00:00:36,230
fundamental pitch factor of sine 75°:
0.966.

11
00:00:36,270 --> 00:00:46,073
Two: for the fifth harmonic, 75° becomes
375°, and the factor is sine 15°: 0.259.

12
00:00:46,113 --> 00:00:52,559
Three: the back-EMF scales with the
winding factor, so the ratio is 0.866.

13
00:00:53,509 --> 00:00:58,651
In 1831, Faraday found that a changing
flux through a coil induces a voltage

14
00:00:58,691 --> 00:01:04,065
in it. Add up the flux through every turn,
and you get the flux linkage, λ.

15
00:01:04,105 --> 00:01:09,524
Faraday's law: the EMF induced around the
coil is minus the rate of change of λ.

16
00:01:09,564 --> 00:01:14,669
The minus sign is Lenz's law: the current
it drives opposes the change.

17
00:01:14,709 --> 00:01:19,399
For a motor, take current as positive into
the terminal.

18
00:01:19,439 --> 00:01:26,118
The terminal voltage is then v = Ri +
dλ/dt, and the back-EMF is e = +dλ/dt.

19
00:01:26,158 --> 00:01:28,731
It's what a voltmeter reads across an open
coil.

20
00:01:28,771 --> 00:01:31,286
We'll use this convention throughout.

21
00:01:32,236 --> 00:01:35,680
In the motor, it's the rotor angle that
changes the flux.

22
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:40,123
Here's Lesson 5's solver, with phase a's
four tooth coils highlighted.

23
00:01:40,163 --> 00:01:45,437
At each rotor position, add the flux
through those four teeth, times six turns.

24
00:01:45,477 --> 00:01:49,787
Stepping the rotor a quarter degree at a
time traces λa against θ:

25
00:01:49,827 --> 00:01:55,097
largest with north magnets on those teeth,
most negative with south magnets.

26
00:01:55,137 --> 00:01:59,317
Its fundamental has an amplitude of 5.94
milliwebers:

27
00:01:59,357 --> 00:02:04,315
λm, the magnet flux linkage, according to
this 2D model.

28
00:02:05,265 --> 00:02:09,497
By the chain rule, the back-EMF is the
slope of λ against θ,

29
00:02:09,537 --> 00:02:12,004
times the electrical speed, ωe.

30
00:02:12,044 --> 00:02:15,131
The slope sets the shape; the speed sets
the size.

31
00:02:15,171 --> 00:02:20,392
Where λa peaks, its slope is zero. The EMF
peaks a quarter cycle earlier,

32
00:02:20,432 --> 00:02:23,358
where λa climbs fastest through zero.

33
00:02:23,398 --> 00:02:30,554
At 1500 rpm, ωe is 4 pole pairs times 157:
628 radians per second.

34
00:02:30,594 --> 00:02:36,763
So the 2D model's fundamental, ωe λm, is
3.73 volts.

35
00:02:37,713 --> 00:02:40,902
How close to a sine is it? Split it into
harmonics.

36
00:02:40,942 --> 00:02:47,160
From Lesson 6, each EMF harmonic is the
winding factor times the field harmonic.

37
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:53,640
Lesson 5's field at the bore has a third
harmonic of 26%, and a fifth near 10%.

38
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:59,055
But a tooth coil spans 120 electrical
degrees, exactly one wavelength of the

39
00:02:59,095 --> 00:03:04,841
third harmonic. Its positive and negative
halves cancel: winding factor zero.

40
00:03:04,881 --> 00:03:08,593
The fifth and seventh pass with 0.866,

41
00:03:08,633 --> 00:03:14,224
the same as the fundamental. Predicted:
9.7% and 3.1%.

42
00:03:14,264 --> 00:03:18,970
The full solver, with slot openings, gives
8.5% and 1.9%:

43
00:03:19,010 --> 00:03:25,172
about 9% distortion. Close to a sine, with
a slightly flattened top.

44
00:03:26,122 --> 00:03:29,928
Without a neutral, a scope sees line-to-
line voltages:

45
00:03:29,968 --> 00:03:33,362
the EMF of phase a minus the EMF of phase
b.

46
00:03:33,402 --> 00:03:37,494
For the fundamental, subtracting two
phasors 120°

47
00:03:37,534 --> 00:03:41,926
apart gives √3 times one of them, leading
by 30°.

48
00:03:41,966 --> 00:03:48,128
For the third harmonic, 120° becomes 360°:
both phases carry the same third,

49
00:03:48,168 --> 00:03:53,570
and it cancels. So does every triplen,
every multiple of three.

50
00:03:53,610 --> 00:03:56,529
That's the factor of a coil spanning 120°,

51
00:03:56,569 --> 00:04:00,281
so our tooth coils had no third harmonic
to lose.

52
00:04:00,321 --> 00:04:04,770
The fifth and seventh survive, but shift
differently from the fundamental,

53
00:04:04,810 --> 00:04:08,586
so the line wave's peak sits 7% above its
fundamental.

54
00:04:09,536 --> 00:04:15,040
The 2D solver gives λm = 5.94 milliwebers.
From here on,

55
00:04:15,080 --> 00:04:21,479
the series uses a nominal 6.5, treated as
measured: 8.6% more.

56
00:04:21,519 --> 00:04:27,147
Part of the gap is the magnets. We assumed
a remanence of 1.2 tesla,

57
00:04:27,187 --> 00:04:32,281
but an N38 datasheet lists 1.22 minimum,
and 1.26 typical.

58
00:04:32,321 --> 00:04:38,728
With linear steel and no current, λ is
exactly proportional to remanence.

59
00:04:38,768 --> 00:04:43,090
So 1.26 tesla gives 6.24 milliwebers: 5%
more.

60
00:04:43,130 --> 00:04:47,148
Closing the gap with remanence alone would
take 1.31 tesla,

61
00:04:47,188 --> 00:04:51,782
beyond this grade. The other 4% belongs to
the third dimension.

62
00:04:51,822 --> 00:04:57,190
A 2D model treats the stack as infinitely
long. Overhanging magnets add flux

63
00:04:57,230 --> 00:05:03,137
at the ends; end leakage removes some. A
3D solution, or the bench, decides.

64
00:05:04,087 --> 00:05:09,186
The shape is a design choice. Here's a
variant, not our reference motor:

65
00:05:09,226 --> 00:05:12,984
180° magnets, and 24 slots with full-pitch
coils.

66
00:05:13,024 --> 00:05:16,341
With full-pitch coils under full-width
magnets,

67
00:05:16,381 --> 00:05:21,711
the phase EMF stays flat for about 130
electrical degrees: a trapezoid.

68
00:05:21,751 --> 00:05:27,714
Its phase EMF has a 29% third harmonic;
between the lines, it vanishes.

69
00:05:27,754 --> 00:05:33,231
That's what a brushless DC drive wants:
current in 120-degree blocks, timed to

70
00:05:33,271 --> 00:05:39,358
the flat tops, gives nearly constant
torque. Lesson 16 compares the two drives.

71
00:05:40,308 --> 00:05:45,493
Datasheets fold λm into a back-EMF
constant, in several conventions.

72
00:05:45,533 --> 00:05:53,409
Phase peak per mechanical radian per
second: p λm, 0.026 volt-seconds.

73
00:05:53,449 --> 00:06:00,155
Line-to-line peak per 1000 rpm: 4.72
volts; in rms, 3.33.

74
00:06:00,195 --> 00:06:04,899
The same number sets the torque. With
current in phase with the back-EMF,

75
00:06:04,939 --> 00:06:09,748
the power it absorbs, 3/2 E I, leaves the
shaft as torque times speed.

76
00:06:09,788 --> 00:06:15,226
So Kt = 3/2 p λm: 0.039 newton-metres per
amp of peak current;

77
00:06:15,266 --> 00:06:22,480
Lesson 9 derives it. A no-current voltage
test gives you the torque per amp.

78
00:06:23,430 --> 00:06:25,745
To measure it, chuck the shaft in a drill,

79
00:06:25,785 --> 00:06:28,513
with the leads disconnected from any
driver.

80
00:06:28,553 --> 00:06:31,661
Clip a scope probe across two leads. The
motor floats,

81
00:06:31,701 --> 00:06:35,225
so the ground clip can go on one. Hold the
speed steady.

82
00:06:35,265 --> 00:06:40,396
Here's a synthetic trace, generated from
the model, of what to expect

83
00:06:40,436 --> 00:06:45,718
near 1500 rpm: about 7 volts peak, one
cycle every 10 milliseconds.

84
00:06:45,758 --> 00:06:50,797
Time several cycles: 10.26 milliseconds
each,

85
00:06:50,837 --> 00:06:57,080
so 97.5 hertz. Times 60, over 4 pole
pairs: 1462 rpm.

86
00:06:57,120 --> 00:07:03,750
For the amplitude, take the fundamental,
from the scope's FFT or a sine fit:

87
00:07:03,790 --> 00:07:09,666
6.90 volts. Then λm is that, over √3 times
ωe: 6.50 milliwebers.

88
00:07:09,706 --> 00:07:13,862
Not the raw peak: on this wave, it reads
about 9% high.

89
00:07:14,812 --> 00:07:18,338
This is a 2D, linear, open-circuit model.
Under load,

90
00:07:18,378 --> 00:07:22,809
armature reaction and tooth saturation
shift the flux linkage.

91
00:07:22,849 --> 00:07:27,761
Magnets also weaken as they warm, about
0.12% per kelvin:

92
00:07:27,801 --> 00:07:32,492
50 kelvin hotter, 6% less λm. Note the
temperature.

93
00:07:32,532 --> 00:07:36,214
And the 3D share of the gap was argued,
not computed.

94
00:07:37,164 --> 00:07:42,806
Three predictions before next time, with
λm = 6.5 milliwebers.

95
00:07:42,846 --> 00:07:48,462
One: at 3000 rpm, what's the peak phase
back-EMF, fundamental only?

96
00:07:49,162 --> 00:07:51,743
Two: and the peak line-to-line EMF?

97
00:07:52,443 --> 00:07:55,893
Three: spin the motor with all three leads
shorted together.

98
00:07:55,933 --> 00:07:59,050
Does it resist, and where does the energy
go?

99
00:07:59,750 --> 00:08:05,323
Next time: energy and co-energy, and how
stored magnetic energy becomes torque.
